     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p3-definition",
     "replacement": "**Linear** equations have a variable with highest power 1.\n\n**Quadratic** equations have a variable with highest power 2, such as $x^2$ (“$x$ squared”).\n\nQuadratic equations can have **up to two real solutions.**\n\nFor $x^2=c$, when $c>0$: Two solutions, a positive and a negative.\tFor $x^2=c$, when $c=0$: One solution, $x=0$.\tFor $x^2=c$, when $c<0$: No real solutions.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p3-definition",
     "targetFingerprint": "9fe3d842073571f4c864c611e0f9d22639fb62d4ee8a526072dfc72222e8d1a5"
    }
   ],
